      ],
      "value": "Evidence for interventions aimed at preventing and treating the common cold is frequently of poor quality, and results are inconsistent. The best evidence for prevention supports physical interventions (e.g., handwashing) and possibly zinc supplements. For traditional treatments, acetaminophen and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs are supported for pain and fever, with ibuprofen appearing superior to acetaminophen for fever in children. Antihistamine-decongestant combinations and intranasal ipratropium may also be beneficial. For nontraditional treatments, oral zinc supplements in adults and honey at bedtime for cough in children over one year show the best evidence."
